Executive summary: We can credibly deliver 200–300 bps of margin improvement and arrest top-line deterioration in 12–18 months by (a) doubling down on 2–3 high-margin customer segments, (b) immediately eliminating discretionary discounting and re-pricing low-value deals, and (c) a two-quarter operational squeeze (service rationalization + targeted cost-to-serve reductions) that preserves growth options while forcing hard portfolio choices. 
Initial narrative (compact): Objectives/constraints — CEO needs near-term profit stabilization without killing growth; runway is 2–4 quarters to show progress ahead of the next earnings cycles and a medium-term repositioning to regain pricing power; Diagnosis — uploaded P&L, CRM cut and NPS point to a small but high-cost cohort (low NPS, high churn, high cost-to-serve) and widespread discount leakage; Hypotheses on where margin and growth come from — enforce disciplined pricing (+~100–150 bps), shift mix toward core profitable segments via retention/up-sell (+~50–100 bps), and cut delivery/SG&A inefficiencies (+~50–100 bps) for a combined 200–300 bps; Tough choices — harvest/exit subscale, price-up or walk from price-driven deals, reallocate sales coverage from low-return accounts, and accelerate product standardization to reduce bespoke delivery. 
Capabilities & competition note: Immediate risk from competitor discount program requires a defensive price/offer response limited to targeted segments (avoid blanket matching); assess sales incentives, operating model flexibility, and IT/ops enablers needed to move customers to standardized propositions quickly. 
24–36 hour analysis plan (by our five-phase methodology): 
- Clarify objectives & constraints: confirm CEO P&L targets, board tolerance for customer exits, acceptable short-term cash vs. margin trade-offs; 
- Segment customers: cleanse CRM, build prioritized segments by revenue, margin, cost-to-serve, NPS/churn and competitor overlap; 
- Value propositions & economics: compute unit economics and contribution margin by segment, model price elasticity and discount waterfall, identify 20–30% of customers/services for migration or exit; 
- Capabilities & competitive position: map delivery cost drivers by account, assess sales compensation and win rates, quantify competitor discount program exposure; 
- Roadmap & impact: build 3 P&L scenarios (base / defensive / aggressive) with 2‑quarter and 12‑18 month impacts (targeting 200–300 bps), list enablers, owners, and key risks. 
Immediate asks for tomorrow’s pre‑brief: validate latest-month CRM, provide deal-level pricing/discount and delivery-cost samples (top 50 accounts), and confirm CEO’s acceptable trade-offs so we can deliver scenario P&Ls and a 90‑day action plan for the client strategy lead.
